1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Se "Slægtebog over afkommet af Anders Sørensen Vedel ved J. Vahl (1896)" [http://dis-danmark.dk/bibliotek/900015.pdf]<br/>
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Født Vejle amt  Nørrevang    Vejle sogn
2 CONT
2 CONT Død Ribe amt   Ribe  Herred  Ribe Domsogn    
2 CONT
2 CONT  Død og eftermæle
2 CONT
2 CONT Vedel døde efter kort tids sygdom, han blev begravet i Ribe Domkirke, hans gravsten er i dag forsvundet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Der står en statue af ham på Kirketorvet foran Skt. Nikolaj Kirke i Vejle, i samme kirke hænger et portræt af ham
2 CONT
2 CONT http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anders_S%C3%B8rensen_Vedel
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.vejlestadsarkiv.dk/dk/default1.asp?what=showlexsite&mode=144
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.denstoredanske.dk/Danmarks_geografi_og_historie/Danmarks_historie/Danske_historikere/Anders_S%C3%B8rensen_Vedel
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders har taget efternavnet Vedel efter sin fødeby Vejle
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Selv skrev han sig Anders Søffrinssøn Vedel, idet han kaldte sig efter sin Fødeby Vejle, hvis Navn i Middelalderen skreves Wæthla, Wæthel og Vedel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den latinske Form for Navnet er Vellejus.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anders Sørensen Vedel'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Søn af Raadmand i Vejle Søren Sørensen og Hustru Sidse, Datter af Borgmester i Vejle Anders Bertelsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1561 Student fra Ribe, 1562 Hovmester for Tyge Brahe, udenlands med denne til 1565; 1566 Mag. i Wittenberg, 1568 Slotspræst i Kbhvn., 1581 Slotspræst i Ribe, 1584—94 kgl. Historiograf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hustru:	Mette Hansdatter,   f. 6 feb. 1561, Ribe Købstad, Ribe, Ribe, Danmark ,   d. 27 dec. 1633, Ribe Købstad, Ribe, Ribe, Danmark   (Alder 72 år) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gift:	20 aug. 1581 	Ribe Købstad, Ribe, Ribe, Danmark   Alder ved brylluppet:	Han var 38 år og 9 måneder - Hun var 20 år og 6 måneder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://finnholbek.dk/getperson.php?personID=I34284&tree=2#cite2
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders Sørensen Vedel was a priest and historian born in Vejle (from which the Vedel name comes), Denmark. He translated the Gesta Danorum by Saxo Grammaticus into Danish 1575.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young man he was the tutor of the astronomer Tycho Brahe on the latter's Grand Tour in Europe becoming the latter's friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1584 Vedel was the official Danish historiographer whose duty was writing the long-expected Latin continuation of Saxo’s work but he overreached himself in his attempt of doing so. He planned an ambitious work in 22 books dea
2 CONC ling with both language and topography as well as with history. However other tasks, lack of help from secretaries, his own slowness and carefulness - and perhaps also a general underestimation of the demands of this work – led to
2 CONC  very modest results and 1594 the task was taken away from him. Much of what he had written here was lost at the fire of Copenhagen 1728.
2 CONT
2 CONT One spin-off from this task however survived namely his edition of a hundred Danish ballads (1591) which became a solid foundation of later knowledge of the elder Danish literary tradition. His own re-creation of some of the balla
2 CONC ds shows his ability as a poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tycho Brahe, born in 1546, was the eldest son of a noble Danish family, and as such appeared destined for the natural aristocratic occupations of hunting and warfare. However, he had an uncle Joergen, a country squire and vice-adm
2 CONC iral, who was more educated, and childless. Tycho's father had agreed with the uncle before Tycho was born that if Tycho was a boy, the uncle could adopt and raise him. He changed his mind and reneged. Then, when a younger brother
2 CONC  was born, the uncle kidnapped Tycho. The father threatened to murder the uncle, but eventually calmed down, since Tycho stood to inherit a large estate from the uncle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Tycho was seven, his uncle insisted that he begin studying Latin. His parents objected, but the uncle said this would help Tycho become a lawyer. At age thirteen, Tycho entered the University of Copenhagen to study law and ph
2 CONC ilosophy. At this impressionable age, an event took place that changed his life. There was a partial eclipse of the sun. This had been predicted, and took place on schedule. It struck Tycho as "something divine that men should kno
2 CONC w the motions of the stars so accurately that they were able a long time beforehand to predict their places and relative positions". Perhaps this predictability was especially appealing to one whose personal life was evolving in r
2 CONC ather an uncertain way. One of the advantages of being a rich kid was that he could immediately go out and buy a copy of Ptolemy's Almagest (in Latin) , and some sets of astronomical tables, which showed the positions of the plane
2 CONC ts at any given time. Ptolemy himself had made such tables, and they had been revised in Spain by a group of fifty astronomers in 1252, brought together by Alfonso X of Castile. These were called the Alfonsine tables. Tycho also b
2 CONC ought a recent set of tables based on Copernicus' theory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At age sixteen, the uncle sent Tycho to Leipzig, in Germany, to continue his study of law. He was accompanied by a tutor, the twenty year old Anders Vedel, who himself later became famous as Denmark's first great historian. Howeve
2 CONC r, Tycho was obsessed with astronomy. He bought books and instruments, which he hid from his tutor, and stayed up much of each night observing the stars. When he was seventeen, he observed a special event---Jupiter and Saturn pass
2 CONC ed very close to each other. (This was on August 17, 1563.) He found on checking the tables that the Alfonsine tables were off by a month in predicting this event, and the Copernicus tables off by several days. Tycho decided this 
2 CONC was a pathetic performance by the astronomers, and much better tables could be constructed just by more accurate observation of the exact positions of the planets over an extended period of time. He decided that this was what he w
2 CONC as going to do. Vedel realized Tycho was a hopeless case, and gave up trying to tutor him in law. The two remained good friends for life. Meanwhile, the uncle died of pneumonia after rescuing the king from drowning after the king 
2 CONC had fallen off the bridge to his castle returning from a naval battle with the Swedes. When Tycho returned to Denmark, the rest of his family were quite unfriendly. They despised his stargazing, and blamed him for neglecting the l
2 CONC aw. He decided to return to Germany, and fell in with some rich amateur astronomers in Augsburg. He persuaded them that what was needed was accurate observation, and (as telescopes had not yet been invented) this meant rather larg
2 CONC e quadrants to get lines of sight on stars. They set up a large wooden quadrant, part of a circle with a nineteen foot radius, that took twenty men to set up. It was graduated in sixtieths of a degree. This was the beginning of Ty
2 CONC cho's accurate observations.
2 CONT
