1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Oluff Jensen Skriver''' (died October 1634) was one of Trondheim's most successful and wealthy merchants during the early 17th century. He had an extensive career as a public official, serving as an alderman (rå
2 CONC dmann), burgomaster (mayor), and chief guardian of minors (overformynder) in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins and Early Career ===
2 CONT No information regarding his parentage or origins has been found in historical sources. He is first recorded in 1607, when he worked as a scribe or clerk at the Trondheim estate (Trondheims gård). His background as a scribe earned
2 CONC  him the byname "Skriver". A few years later, on February 21, 1614, he is registered as a burgher of Trondheim in connection with chartering a merchant vessel from Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Public Offices and Controversies ===
2 CONT * '''Alderman:''' He was appointed as an alderman in Trondheim around 1615, when he first appears in that role. In May 1616, he joined the lagman (lawspeaker) in conducting an official inventory of the Trondheim estate. In 1619, h
2 CONC e traveled to Stavanger alongside town scribe Mikkel Lauritsen to legally defend the municipal privileges of Trondheim.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster:''' Oluff Jensen is first titled as burgomaster in 1618 and appears frequently in this office over the following years (including 1619 and 1621). However, his mayoral tenure was marked by friction. In March 1622, 
2 CONC the fief lord (lensherre) noted that Oluff Jensen faced multiple accusations («uti adskillige maader at beskyldes») and ordered a formal investigation. While the primary sources do not detail the exact charges, they indicate he ac
2 CONC ted in an overly autocratic manner. For instance, in February 1623, he was prosecuted for issuing an official passport under the town's seal without consulting or informing the rest of the city council.
2 CONT * '''Chief Guardian and Later Duties:''' Following his time as burgomaster, he was appointed chief guardian of minors (overformynder) in Trondheim on January 13, 1625, a position he likely held for the remainder of his life. In Au
2 CONC gust 1632, he sat on a royal commission tasked with auditing the tax rolls of the Namdal bailiff, Peder Lauritsen.
2 CONT * '''Churchwarden of Nidaros Cathedral:''' From 1619 to 1632, he served as the churchwarden (kirkeverge) for Nidaros Cathedral. He personally funded a new roof over the western nave out of his own pocket; in gratitude for this sub
2 CONC stantial donation, he was granted a burial plot inside the cathedral itself. According to E.C. Tønder's genealogies, he passed away in October 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Wealth and Commercial Enterprise ===
2 CONT Oluff Skriver was a highly affluent businessman with substantial commercial revenues. In 1630, he paid the servant tax (drengeskatt) for five male servants, which was the highest number recorded for any burgher in the city. His co
2 CONC mmercial interests included:
2 CONT * '''Shipping and Timber Trade:''' He engaged in large-scale shipping operations. In 1614, he chartered a 36-lester vessel from Ystad and a 35-lester Dutch ship laden with malt and oats. In 1626, he freighted a Dutch vessel with s
2 CONC awn timber from his own sawmill operation in Hemne, which he co-owned with Johan de Nouquer.
2 CONT * '''Herring and Brick Trade:''' He supplied large quantities of herring to the fief lord, such as in April 1628 when he sold 40 lester of herring for 1,500 riksdaler. He also appeared regularly in the herring tithe registries for
2 CONC  Bjugn. Furthermore, both Oluff and his widow dealt in building materials; as late as 1646, his widow sold 1,000 glazed bricks for the reconstruction of the bishop's residence.
2 CONT * '''Real Estate and Sawmills:''' He owned a town estate in Trondheim. Additionally, he held multiple sawmills in Hemne and the Møre region, alongside an extensive portfolio of rural lands in Skogn, Strinda, and Gauldal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his death, his widow engaged in significant charitable works. On March 30, 1648, she donated 200 riksdaler to the city's poor. This capital consisted of funds originally loaned out to the impoverished by Oluff Skriver. T
2 CONC he interest from this donation was structured to yield 100 riksdaler for the shelterless poor and 100 riksdaler for the institution known as Kierlingeuset. In 1652, Laurits Stabell sold a house in Trondheim on behalf of the deceas
2 CONC ed widow's family.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Oluff Jensen Skriver was married to '''Aarsille Christoffersdatter''' (died November 20, 1648). She was potentially a daughter of burgomaster Christoffer Biørnsen and Sophia von Ravensborg. (Older claims by the historian Daae sugg
2 CONC esting that Oluff remarried a woman named Valborg Jensdatter after Aarsille's death are incorrect, as Oluff died several years before his wife).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT * '''Sussana Olufsdatter:''' Married on August 2, 1623, to Reverend Peder Lauritsen Krabbe of Aure.
2 CONT * '''Karen Olufsdatter:''' Married in 1631 to the bailiff of Nordmøre, Christoffer Nilsen Tønder.
2 CONT * '''Christoffer Olufsen:''' Documented in the fines and penalties registry (sakefall) for 1638/39, where he paid a fine concerning an illegitimate relationship with a girl from Nordfjord, Maren Andersdatter.
2 CONT * '''Jens Olsen (?):''' It is highly probable that the Jens Olsen who later worked as a scribe at the Trondheim estate was Oluff's son.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', pp. 31–32 (section "Oluff Jensen Skriver"). Based on primary sources including the Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR Vol. VI), Court Records of the Norwegian 
2 CONC Diet (NHD), local fief accounts, and Nidaros Cathedral financial books. Referenced from images {A7A0C31C-42B9-4C1A-866E-6D4201C7AEC3}.jpg and {66CF7229-8E0F-4BB8-A15E-17DD5766E15F}.jpg.
2 CONT * UBIT: E.C. Tønder's family lineage charts (manuscript collection).
